   "component_id": "ED26-P15-CH-regular-conditional-laws-C011",
   "verdict": "partial",
   "gap": "The source's framing is absent: Durrett presents the sphere example as Borel's paradox (p. 210): at first glance the longitude and the angle along the great circle should both be uniform, yet the angle is not, and the paradox evaporates because in either coordinate system the angle is independent of the longitude. The entry gives the densities and the independence but neither names the example Borel's paradox (the name appears only in passing in bayes-factor-computation) nor states the apparent paradox it resolves.",
