  {
   "component_id": "ED26-P15-CH-regular-conditional-laws-C012",
   "verdict": "partial",
   "gap": "Durrett (p. 214) records that regular conditional distributions do not always exist, attributes the first example to Dieudonne (1948) with references to Doob (1953, p. 624) and Faden (1985), and explains the source of the problem: countable additivity holds almost surely for each disjoint sequence, but the exceptional sets can pile up. The route has only the bare nonexistence statement (def:p15-w02b-p15-008-limits item 3) and the remark that the exceptional set may depend on the event (def:p15-regular-conditional-law); the attribution and the explanation are absent.",
